Blindness
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Visual acuity <3/60 or no light perception, Absent pupillary light reflex, Roving eye movements, Severe optic disc pallor

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-007
Reason for Visit: Acute fall with secondary trauma and profound panic due to environmental disorientation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated the patient sustained a heavy fall over relocated furniture, resulting in a deep shin laceration and an acute panic attack. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because the laceration required immediate closure to prevent infection, and the patient's severe distress required acute medical stabilization and environmental risk mitigation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I tripped over a chair that wasn't supposed to be there, tore my leg open, and now I am completely terrified to move.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a congenital onset history of complete absence of light perception. Due to a recent disruption in the home's layout by a visiting relative, the patient experienced an inability to navigate the unfamiliar environment without assistance. One hour ago, relying on tactile and auditory cues that were suddenly inaccurate, the patient tripped over a relocated chair, sustaining a painful injury to the right lower leg. This resulted in an acute panic attack, with the patient refusing to move from the floor due to profound spatial disorientation and fear. The patient reports a severely altered circadian rhythm recently, contributing to fatigue and reduced spatial awareness today.
Functional Status: Usually independent with a guide dog; currently immobile on the floor due to fear and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Complete disruption of the trusted sensory environment; high risk of recurrent falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70.0 Kg
General: Patient is sitting on the floor, visibly trembling, diaphoretic, and hyperventilating.
Physical Exam: Ocular exam confirms visual acuity of no light perception bilaterally. There is an absent pupillary light reflex and continuous roving eye movements. Fundoscopy, where briefly visualised, shows severe optic disc pallor. The right anterior tibia exhibits a 4cm gaping laceration through the dermis with active venous oozing; no underlying bone exposed or crepitus. Neurological exam reveals normal gross motor and sensory function in the limbs.
Environmental Observations: Furniture is haphazardly placed; the patient's guide dog is secured in another room.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; tetanus immunization status confirmed up to date.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Blindness (complicated by acute mechanical fall, tibial laceration, and situational panic) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Tibial Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the ability to weight-bear briefly with assistance, lack of bony step-off, and absence of extreme localized bony tenderness.

Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to the clear mechanical mechanism of tripping over an object and lack of prodromal dizziness or loss of consciousness.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely given the chest pain is absent and the tachycardia and tachypnea are clearly proportional to the acute panic and pain of the fall.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a gaping lower limb laceration complicated by severe spatial panic in a visually impaired individual mandated urgent in-home evaluation. The objective findings of a 4cm dermis-deep wound actively bleeding confirmed the need for immediate suturing. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because transporting a completely disoriented and panicked patient to an ED would have caused extreme psychological trauma, and the open wound required prompt primary closure to minimize infection risk.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered local infiltration of 1% Lignocaine. Wound cleaned, irrigated, and closed with 4 interrupted non-absorbable sutures. Applied a sterile compression dressing. Provided verbal reassurance to terminate the panic attack.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the household uses verbal confirmations whenever an object is moved, you map the environment accurately; therefore, enforcing a strict "no silent moving" rule builds spatial confidence. When you explicitly trace the edges of the room with your cane after visitors leave, you reset your internal map; executing this mapping routine restores your independent mobility.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to Vision Australia for an urgent home safety and orientation assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended tactile markers for floor transitions.
Follow-up: GP review in 7 days for wound check and suture removal.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the wound begins to leak yellow pus, becomes excessively hot and red, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Wound healing is subject to adequate rest and absence of infection; visually impaired patients may have difficulty self-monitoring the wound.
Clinical Course: The laceration should heal over 7-10 days. The acute panic will subside as the environment is systematically returned to its baseline state.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Have your spouse physically inspect the bandage twice a day. Seek immediate care if the pain becomes throbbing and unmanageable with paracetamol.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sutures pull the skin edges together to stop the bleeding and allow the tissue to knit back together. A secure environment is necessary to prevent a recurrence.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing clean and dry. Instruct all visitors not to move furniture. Use the cane to re-map the house tomorrow.

